The baseline does one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add (the add costs about 2.5 ms for encoding). The circuit is already minimal: y = x*A + B needs one ct-pt multiply, and B has to enter somehow.

One idea is to avoid the rescale by folding B in differently. For example, `(x + B/A) * A` would need a plaintext division, which isn't allowed, and it would still need the multiply and rescale.

Another idea is to change the order so the add happens at a different level. Adding B after the rescale uses a plaintext at the normal scale, which is what the baseline already does. Adding B before the multiply would need B/A, which can't be computed.

The remaining option is a different circuit structure that CHEHAB might evaluate more cheaply. The plaintext-add encoding cost is unavoidable because B is a plaintext vector input. Multiplying a ciphertext by a plaintext and then adding is the same circuit however I write it. So I'll make one attempt with an explicit subtraction form, `y = x*A - (-B)`. This cannot help, because negating a plaintext is plaintext arithmetic and is not allowed.

I'll resubmit the baseline form. It is likely optimal, and any variant will probably be normalized to the same circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline circuit looks minimal: one ct-pt multiply with its rescale, plus one plaintext add. I can't fold B in before the multiply, since that needs B/A and plaintext arithmetic is not allowed. I also can't drop the rescale. This attempt uses in-place accumulation (`y *= a; y += b`) in case CHEHAB lowers it to a different circuit. It will probably normalize to the same one.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x;
  y *= a;
  y += b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I don't see a cheaper circuit. Any exact evaluation of x*A + B needs one ct-pt multiply with its rescale, plus one plaintext add. I'm resubmitting the same form instead of a variant that adds operations.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

There is no cheaper exact circuit that I can see. This attempt builds B into a ciphertext at the fresh level and adds it to x*A. It is probably no faster, but it changes how the plaintext add is scheduled relative to the rescale.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext z = x - x;
  z += b;
  Ciphertext y = x * a;
  y += z;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I still see no cheaper exact circuit. CHEHAB folded the x - x trick away in the last attempt. The baseline's one ct-pt multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add look like the minimum for this function. I'm resubmitting that form.

```cpp
